045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186497477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1045 "The Circumference of a Circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7b10485d-7fa4-5254-83ce-37fba428298f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069256 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069256 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32097" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069256 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069256 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/af/afcd7f6646b84debcea24ad6bf3a9cd71d14aae778cdb31b77a36665865a1996.png)\n\nWhat is the circumference of a circle with a radius of $8\\,\\text{cm}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069256 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81faa1ed-4997-5c7e-9226-5ab40a6765e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069256 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069256 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the formula $C=2\\pi r$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 8 \\\\  & =16\\pi \\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069431 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069431 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069431 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069431 #edb/ref 1071 "Ken's tree has a diameter of $4\\,\\text{ft}.$ What length of chain does Ken need for it to wrap around the tree perfectly?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069431 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fab21302-ee94-5b6f-a645-ce41d0356979" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069431 #edb/ref 1074 "The length of the chain needed to wrap around the tree is its circumference.\n\nThe formula for the circumference of a circle is $C=\\pi d,$ where $d=4\\,\\text{ft}$ is the diameter.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nC=\\pi \\cdot 4=4\\pi \\,\\text{ft}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069547 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069547 #edb/ref 1061 10876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069547 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3437ac3b-f6ef-5e78-8b46-bca7d485abd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069547 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069548 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069548 #edb/ref 1061 10877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069548 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "51b49060-4f8e-51b6-a0fb-696a9394fe72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069548 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069549 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069549 #edb/ref 1061 10878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069549 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f70e49e-370a-5a2c-9320-28d0225c9e1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069549 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069550 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069550 #edb/ref 1061 10879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069550 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9fe7dd8-677c-508f-836a-f00b1739d9cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069550 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069551 #edb/ref 1061 10881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069551 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fb4b1e1c-564b-5857-b040-cc788b2e5510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069551 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069956 #edb/ref 1064 "The **circumference** of a circle is the length of the path that goes around the circle.\n\n![](images/ac/ac8f31a99961c51cb53695d9daab017126d1316b720e6383b876824d7c7947bd.png)\n\nThe circumference is usually denoted by the letter $C.$\n\nOne important fact about the circumference is that, for *any* circle, no matter how small or large it is, the ratio of circumference $C$ to diameter $d$ is a fixed number! Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{C}{d} = {\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{\\pi}} \\approx 3.141\\,592\\,654...\n$$\n\n \n\nThis important irrational number is called **pi** (pronounced \"pie\") and we use the Greek letter $\\pi$ to express it. So, we can calculate the circumference by using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=\\pi d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, our circle has diameter $4\\,\\textrm{cm},$ so its circumference is\n\n$$\nC = \\pi \\cdot 4 = 4\\pi \\approx 12.566\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal digits.\n\nSince the diameter is twice as long as the radius ($d=2r$), we can also write the formula for the circumference as\n\n$$\nC = \\pi d = 2\\pi r.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069956 #edb/ref 1065 2423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069956 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e75a0c81-7a99-51d8-85ff-de6951125d7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069956 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497477 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497477 #edb/ref 1061 643569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497477 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc9d4179-5034-59aa-b0e6-47636130b8db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497477 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186497490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497490 #edb/ref 1064 "The problem of finding the length of a circle or a part of a circle often appears in real life. \n\nFor example, consider the pie in the shape of a circle divided into six equal slices, as shown in the figure below.\n\n![](images/48/48b416b9dffbf6e3e746e3fa8f90ec8043f937fe612e36e1219b5e9e8c368f22.png)\n\n How to find the total length of the border of one slice of pie?\n\nThe border of the slice of pie will consist of two parts:\n\n- the top and the bottom parts, which consist of two radii;\n\n- the right part (circular part), which consists of one-sixth of the circumference.\n\nFirst, let's find the length of the circular part. The formula for the circumference of a circle is ![](images/63/636374af24eb4d5c435de97b8e1ac460148deba4916b0954e3d1d2f7a364f9d7.svg) where ![](images/31/3122e2ea79ab0512edfce5c875a099de1a7067581b658d1b8fc9c105277aed36.svg) is the radius. Therefore, we have \n![](images/15/15bff81cb45d3df680e782e69534d2489388dfbcfd12b3cd579298d03f8bd452.svg)\n\nFinally, the length of the border is\n![](images/15/15037adbdea6b21d54991917b5dad6108c60257fd8f509300642343a101ebeeb.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497490 #edb/ref 1065 15549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497490 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a14139c8-87eb-5e69-a268-c7cb088cf812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497490 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Length of a Part of a Circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545195 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186545879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545195 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186545974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545195 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186546021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545195 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545195 #edb/ref 1045 "The Circumference of a Circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545195 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186545974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545195 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545195 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545195 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ddf2c666-86f8-502f-9585-efc495d0f82c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545208 #edb/ref